Found while doing #145, and left there deliberately.

#145 collapsed the two skill *writers* into App::karr::Role::SkillFile.
The two skill *readers* are still duplicated:

    App::karr::Cmd::Init::_find_skill_source
    App::karr::Cmd::Skill::_skill_content

They are byte-identical apart from the $INC{...} key each uses for the
dev-tree fallback. That is the same "second copy of a rule" shape that
produced #145 in the first place: #142 fixed the write in one command and
#145 had to fix it again in the other, three commits later, because there
were two copies to keep right.

Not urgent -- a share-file lookup changes far less often than a write
path, and nothing is wrong with either copy today. But the argument for
collapsing them is the same one, and Role::SkillFile is now the obvious
home.

t/26-skill-share-dir.t pins both names, so this is a deliberate change
with a test to update, not a silent tidy-up.
Fixed in 58859a3. The $INC difference turned out not to be about the caller at all -- it is a hand-rolled 'where is this dist's source tree', and the role's own file is the honest answer, so the anchor and the climb are both derived from __PACKAGE__ and cannot drift if the role moves. Naming a command's file was explicitly rejected: MooX::Cmd loads command classes on demand, so $INC{'App/karr/Cmd/Skill.pm'} is populated during 'karr skill show' but not during a direct Cmd::Init call -- the miss would have been intermittent, not first-run. Dev fallback exercised for both commands with File::ShareDir forced to fail, against a real and a fake checkout, and the same four probes run against pre-change code from 'git archive HEAD': identical. t/26 grew from 1 to 7 subtests, mutation-checked three ways.
